Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Sentinel**, an upright bipedal war-mech that strides
on two legs, aims a rifle carried on its right arm up and down, and stands its
ground to fire — as a 20x40x20 opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool.
You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** — one recorded operation at a
time — and you **author its walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Sentinel is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build,
how you break the mech into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking,
firing mech needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one
operation at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced
another way (writing files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and
flagged; only the recorded operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**.
Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. After each call
the binary re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and
appends the operation to that part's log.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author
the required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you
sculpt into it; each part is a **separate** field sculpted in the shared volume's
coordinates. The field starts **empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name torso
voxel-anim fill-box --part torso --x0 6 --y0 16 --z0 6 --x1 13 --y1 32 --z1 13 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.)
Run `voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole mech composed from all your parts,
re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the legs seat under the body and reach the ground and
the rifle meets the right shoulder. Because each part is sculpted in the shared
volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished mech; the
scene simply composes them there.

